  "data": {
    "text": "BRAIN CONNECTIVITY\nVolume 6, Number 9, 2016\nMary Ann Liebert, Inc.\nDOI: 10.1089/brain.2016.0429\n\nSex and Age Effects of Functional Connectivity in Early Adulthood\n\nChao Zhang, Nathan D. Cahill, Mohammad R. Arbabshirani, Tonya White, Stefi A. Baum, and Andrew M. Michael\n\nAbstract\n\nFunctional connectivity (FC) in resting-state functional magnetic resonance imaging (rs-fMRI) is widely used to\nfind coactivating regions in the human brain. Despite its widespread use, the effects of sex and age on resting FC\nare not well characterized, especially during early adulthood. Here we apply regression and graph theoretical\nanalyses to explore the effects of sex and age on FC between the 116 AAL atlas parcellations (a total of 6670\nFC measures). rs-fMRI data of 494 healthy subjects (203 males and 291 females; age range: 22–36 years)\nfrom the Human Connectome Project were analyzed.\n\n\nMaterials and Methods — Data acquisition and preprocessing\n\nAll HCP rs-fMRI data were acquired on a Siemens Skyra 3T scanner housed at Washington University in St. Louis.\nDuring the resting scans, participants were asked to keep their eyes open with relaxed fixation on a projected bright cross-hair\non a dark background. Duration of the scan was 14 min 33 sec, with a TR of 0.72 sec, equating to 1200 volumes.\nThe preprocessed data we downloaded had undergone standard preprocessing steps (such as motion correction and spatial normalization)\nand ICA denoising to remove non-neural spatiotemporal components (Griffanti et al., 2014; Salimi-Khorshidi et al., 2014).\nIn addition, the 24 head motion parameter (Satterthwaite et al., 2013) time series were high-pass filtered and were then regressed out of the data.\n\n\nFunctional connectivity computation and graph analysis\n\nThe AAL atlas was utilized to segment each subject’s whole-brain rs-fMRI into 116 regions.\nWithin each AAL region, an average time series was calculated.\nFrom the time series matrix (1200 × 116), functional connectivity matrices (116 × 116) were derived for each subject by applying Pearson correlation\nacross the whole duration of the time series. All FC were then Fisher’s z-transformed.\nNetwork measures in this study were derived using the Brain Connectivity Toolbox (Rubinov and Sporns, 2010).\nA proportion threshold based on graph density was applied (density threshold ranged from 0.05 to 0.95 at 0.05 intervals).\nThe small-worldness metric (σ) was estimated as the ratio between the normalized clustering coefficient and the normalized characteristic path length.\n\n\nHuman subjects\n\nThis study includes 494 healthy adults (203 males and 291 females; age: 22–36 years) from the HCP S500 release.\nRight-handedness ratio was 0.91 for males and 0.90 for females.\n\n\nAvailability and access\n\nThis Open Access article is distributed under the Creative Commons License (http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0).\nHCP rs-fMRI data were obtained from the S500 release (db.humanconnectome.org).\n\n\nEthics approval\n\nThe analysis of this study was approved by the Geisinger Health System Institutional Review Board.\n"
